course: dictionary
lesson: 8814
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sheep is a farm animal with a thick woolly coat and 4 legs. Farmers keep sheep for wool, meat and milk. You will see the word in farm stories, in maths sums about animals, and in science lessons about lambs and shearing.
explain: Sheep is special because it does not change when there is more than one. «One sheep, two sheep.» You know how many there are from the number, or from the small words "is" and "are": «The sheep is in the field» means one, and «The sheep are in the field» means more than one.
explain: Some words have a second meaning. A person who just copies others is sometimes called a sheep. Context tells you which meaning is right: «The farmer counted his sheep before dark» is about animals, but «Don't be a sheep, think for yourself» is about people. Always read the words around the word before you choose.
fact: Some words don't change at all: one sheep, two sheep; one deer, two deer.
fact: «Farm sheep grow wool all the time and must be shorn every year.» Shorn means had their wool cut off.
fact: A sheep called Shrek in New Zealand escaped shearing for six years and grew 27 kilograms of wool.
fact: After 1066 English kept the animal words cow, pig and sheep, but the meat from the French-speaking lord's table was called beef, pork and mutton, from French bœuf, porc and mouton.
fact: People also milk sheep, and feta cheese is usually made from sheep's milk.
exercise: «One sheep, two sheep.» Why does sheep stay the same for more than one? | It means the sheep are lost | It is a spelling mistake that needs an s | *Some words don't change at all | Two farmers share the word
exercise: «Farm sheep must be shorn every year.» What does shorn mean? | Fed with extra grass | Painted with bright colours | *Had their wool cut off | Given a bath in the river
exercise: «Don't be a sheep, think for yourself.» What does sheep mean here? | A farm animal with a woolly coat | A kind of cheese | A large bell on a farm | *A person who just copies others
test: After 1066 the meat on the lord's table was called mutton. Which French word did it come from? | *mouton | bœuf | moutard | porc
test: Which animal's milk is usually used to make feta cheese? | horse | cow | goat | *sheep
exam: How many legs does a sheep have? | two | three | six | *four
